<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Layla and Majnun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1188</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Layla and Majnun is a Persian narrative poem by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizami-ganjavi">Nizami Ganjavi</a>, completed in 1188 at the request of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshah</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhsitan-i">Akhsitan I</a>. Based on an Arabic tale of the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qays">Qays</a>, driven mad by his hopeless love for Layla, it became one of the best-known love stories of the Islamic world. It forms part of Nizami's Khamsa and inspired many later versions in Persian, Turkic and other languages.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/layla-and-majnun.jpg" alt="Layla and Majnun" width="320" height="518" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Layla_and_Majnun2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
